{#- The redirect table Apache serves 301s from: a mod_rewrite `txt:` map, read by
    RewriteMap species_redirect in the beeatlas.net vhosts (both :80 and :443 —
    port 80 serves the site directly rather than redirecting to HTTPS).

    It ships in the docroot because that is what the publish step swaps into
    place, so a synonym added to a seed reaches Apache through an ordinary deploy
    with no root access and no config edit. mod_rewrite re-reads a txt map when
    its mtime changes, so no reload is needed either. HTTP access to the file
    itself is denied in the vhost — mod_rewrite reads it from disk, not over the
    network.

    The HTML redirect pages (_pages/species-redirect.njk) stay, from the same
    list, as the fallback: if this map is missing, stale, or the vhost is ever
    rebuilt without the rules, the reader is still forwarded — just by meta
    refresh instead of a 301. Two mechanisms, one source. -#}
# BeeAtlas species redirects — GENERATED, do not edit.
# Source: data/dbt/seeds/*synonyms*.csv via _data/synonyms.js.
# Consumed by: RewriteMap species_redirect (beeatlas.net vhost).
# Format: <old-path> <accepted-path>, one per line.
{% for r in synonyms.redirects -%}
/species/{{ r.fromSlug }}/ /species/{{ r.toSlug }}/index.html
{% endfor -%}
